      "latency_ms": 2035.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 776,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, enabling chemical reactions in biological systems.<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, allowing insects to walk on water and droplets to form. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Water reaches its maximum density at ~4 °C; colder water expands, which is why ice floats. This protects aquatic life in winter. |\n| **pH** | Pure water self‑ionizes: 2 H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻, giving a neutral pH of 7 at 25 °C. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of the planet’s water is saline ocean water; the rest is freshwater in glaciers, groundwater, lakes, and the atmosphere). |\n| **Biological role** | • Solvent and medium for metabolic reactions.<br>• Transporter of nutrients and waste in blood, sap, and cellular fluids.<br>• Reactant in photosynthesis (splits into O₂ and H⁺).<br>• Structural component (e.g., turgor pressure in plant cells). |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), cooling, chemical manufacturing, food processing, cleaning, recreation, etc. |\n\n### Why water is special\n1. **Polarity & Hydrogen Bonding** – Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with neighbors, creating a dynamic network that gives water its high cohesion, surface tension, and unusual thermodynamic properties.\n2. **Thermal stability** – The large heat capacity buffers temperature fluctuations, making Earth’s climate relatively stable.\n3. **Solvent versatility** – Because many substances dissolve in water, it serves as the “universal medium” for chemical and biological processes.\n4. **Phase flexibility** – The ability to exist in all three common states under Earth‑like conditions enables the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation), which redistributes heat and nutrients globally.\n\n### Quick summary\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonding molecule (H₂O) that can be solid, liquid, or gas near Earth’s surface temperatures. Its unique physical and chemical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, density anomaly, and excellent solvency—make it indispensable for climate regulation, life’s chemistry, and countless human activities."
